ource ecosystems starve. A congressional staffer looking at the 2023 Executive Order on Artificial Intelligence sees a compliance framework that entrenches market concentration under the guise of national security.\n\nThe human cost of regulatory delay is measured in preventable deaths and stagnant productivity rather than averted science-fiction catastrophes. Every month that the FTC and NIST spend debating weight thresholds and mandatory know-your-customer rules for compute providers is a month patients wait for automated cures for diseases like glioblastoma and pancreatic cancer. The political coalition supporting these restrictions unites legacy software monopolies with risk-averse bureaucrats who face zero career downside for inaction and infinite downside for false negatives. Conversely, the coalition opposing restrictions consists of independent research labs, university consortia, and open-source developers who bear the direct economic cost of compliance paralysis. When regulators choose to freeze development out of hypothetical fear, they are explicitly choosing to let people die today of diseases we could automate a cure for. Congress must reject any statutory framework that restricts open weights, and instead direct agencies to focus enforcement strictly on malicious downstream behavior rather than upstream distribution.",
